# pond/enclosure_policy.kyri -- what the agent-jail lap declares today, written as a value.
#
# ACCRETE-ONLY. This record READS tools/ag/agent-jail.sh and tools/e/enclosure.conf.example and
# says what they build. It rewrites neither, and it flips nothing: the ENCLOSURE selector stays
# ai-jail until the switchover round lands behind its audit.
#
# Paths follow tools/e/enclosure.conf.example's own placeholder convention -- /home/youruser and
# its project bind -- because the real tools/e/enclosure.conf is gitignored per host. The shapes
# are exact; only the handle stands in. The four state binds read `loops/<body>/`, the one room
# read-scope.md seated on 20260827; agent-jail.sh adopts an elder `.claude-state` on next launch.
#
# THE NETWORK LINE, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED (20260828, REDS %329). This record read
# `network off` from its first day. It is refuted three ways on this pier: the jail's own network
# namespace is byte-identical to the host's (`net:[4026531833]` read from /proc/<pid>/ns/net for a
# live jailed agent and for the host shell beside it, while their PID namespaces differ, so the
# reading can tell a shared namespace from an unshared one); `ai-jail --help` offers no network
# option at all, only --tailscale, --ssh, and --pictures; and neither agent-jail.sh nor
# enclosure.conf.example spells a network flag. So the enclosure this record describes carries
# full host network, and no flag the launcher could pass would close it.
#
# `network on` is therefore the honest declaration, and it makes this policy read HOLD rather than
# PLACE -- which is the module's own design working. An open network is check-in territory by the
# quest's custody line, so `on` waits for a word instead of passing quietly. The lap wants the
# network (an agent that cannot reach its API does nothing), and now the record says so out loud
# where an operator reads it.
#
# THE JAIL'S OWN DEFAULTS, MEASURED 20260828 (orbit two's first lap). Until this lap the record
# declared six mounts, and the enclosure builds twenty-seven. The missing twenty-one come from
# ai-jail's own defaults rather than from any flag the launcher passes, so
# tools/fixtures/p/pond_policy_launcher_scan.sh -- which compares this record against what the
# launcher SPELLS -- could not see them, and said so in its own header. They are declared below,
# read off `ai-jail --dry-run`, which prints the bwrap plan without starting anything. The
# instrument is tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_built_scan.sh and the measurement it read is
# pinned at tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_default_plan.kyri.
#
# THREE OF THOSE LINES DO NOT PLACE, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. `map /sys` refuses, because this
# module's own roster refuses a whole-/sys map by name -- it hands back exactly what the
# namespaces were raised to hold -- and the enclosure read-only-binds all of it. The four `mask`
# lines under it close the classic corners (firmware, the security and debug interfaces, the fuse
# control), and a reading that could weigh a mask against the map above it would be a real
# improvement and a LOOSENING, so it waits for a word rather than being written by the lap that
# found the problem. `rw-map /tmp/.X11-unix:/tmp/.X11-unix` refuses for the roster's other line:
# a shared host /tmp, here the X11 socket directory every X client on the host can be reached
# through, which arrives because the launcher passes no --no-display. `device /dev/shm` holds, as
# every device outside the seated GPU lane does.
#
# So this record now reads REFUSE, exit 2, naming the first line to open. That is the module
# working rather than a regression -- the same shape as `network on` holding a week earlier. Pond
# will not admit this enclosure as it stands, and orbit two derives its grants from a record that
# says so out loud.
#
# `map /nix/store` LEFT WITH THIS LAP. It was the record's honest guess at a jail default and the
# plan says the default is `/nix`, whole. One right line replaces one near-miss.
#
# THE DOOR'S FIRST DUTY, SEATED 20260829. Every mark in this record until now described the ROOM
# -- what an agent inside can see, write, or reach. Three duties describe the DOOR instead: which
# program starts (`entry`), what environment it carries (`env`), and which user it runs as
# (`user`). The grammar could state none of them, which is orbit four's own measure of distance,
# counted by tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh. `user` is seated first because it is the
# cheapest to check and the one an unattended lap already leans on.
#
# `invoking` RATHER THAN A NUMBER, MEASURED RATHER THAN PROPOSED. The reading that opened this door
# (active-designing/20260829-064107_the-door-the-record-cannot-name.md) proposed one key and one
# integer. Three measurements on this pier refute the integer, and they are the same three that
# refuted `network off` above: tools/ag/agent-jail.sh spells no uid flag on its exec line,
# `ai-jail --help` offers no uid or user option at all, and the door guard's probe leg read the
# agent running at the invoking user's own uid. A number here would be a claim nothing keeps --
# which is the REDS %329 fault exactly, one mark over. So the record declares what the launcher
# does: the enclosure runs the agent as whoever opened the door.
#
# `root` REFUSES BY NAME, because tools/ag/agent-jail.sh already tells an operator the opposite in
# its own words -- claude refuses --dangerously-skip-permissions at uid 0, so run the jail as a
# non-root user. That note is a comment somebody hopes is read; this line is a thing a program
# checks.
#
# THE DOOR'S SECOND DUTY, SEATED 20260830. `env` names what the launcher hands across the
# threshold, one variable per line, written the way the exec line writes it -- `KEY=value` -- so an
# operator comparing it with tools/ag/agent-jail.sh reads the same bytes on both sides. The two
# lines below are that exec line's own assignments in this record's placeholder namespace, and
# tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh settles them against it at zero. `entry` stays last
# on purpose: naming the program inside the record is the switchover, which is Keaton's word. The
# mark's own reasoning lives beside verdict_for_env in pond/enclosure_policy.rye.
#
# `GH_CONFIG_DIR` POINTS AT A CREDENTIAL ROOM AND PLACES, which is this seat's finding. An env value
# is a pointer the agent follows once inside; `persist /home/youruser/grain` above is the mount that
# carries `.gh` across, as an ancestor. Adding `.gh` to the key-room roster would refuse the pointer
# and leave that door open -- a wall on the sign rather than on the room. Its own lap.
#
# AND THE BOUND READS THIS COMMENT. max_policy_len is 8,192 bytes over the whole document, and the
# header is most of it; `entry`'s paragraph will meet that wall. A record past the bound now refuses
# by name rather than being read in part (REDS `20260830.032600`), so the wall is loud when it arrives.
#
#   pond/bin/enclosure-policy check pond/enclosure_policy.kyri
format pond-enclosure-policy-v1
name agent-jail-lap
private-home yes
network on
gpu no
user invoking
env GH_CONFIG_DIR=/home/youruser/grain/.gh
env PATH=/run/current-system/sw/bin:/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin:/home/youruser/.nix-profile/bin:/bin
map /run/current-system
persist /home/youruser/grain
ephemeral /tmp

# The jail's own defaults, in the order the plan builds them.
map /usr
map /bin
map /lib64
map /nix
map /etc
map /opt
map /sys
fresh /dev
fresh /proc
ephemeral /run
graft /nix/store/<hash>-hosts
graft /etc/resolv.conf
graft /tmp/.ai-jail-landlock
mask /sys/firmware
mask /sys/kernel/security
mask /sys/kernel/debug
mask /sys/fs/fuse
device /dev/shm
rw-map /tmp/.X11-unix:/tmp/.X11-unix
rw-map /run/user/<uid>:/run/user/<uid>
rw-map /home/youruser/grain/loops/claude:/home/youruser/.claude
rw-map /home/youruser/grain/loops/cursor:/home/youruser/.cursor
rw-map /home/youruser/grain/loops/cursor/xdg-config:/home/youruser/.config/cursor
rw-map /home/youruser/grain/loops/codex:/home/youruser/.codex
rw-map /home/youruser/grain/loops/claude/dot-claude.json:/home/youruser/.claude.json
